&lt;h2&gt;


📍 This Week's Opportunities
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are a few opportunities I came across this week that I thought were worth sharing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


📌 HackerRank Orchestrate
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who it's for:&lt;/strong&gt; Developers, students, and builders who want to try building an AI agent in a short, focused challenge. It's open to anyone, and the challenge is &lt;strong&gt;solo&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What stands out:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;HackerRank Orchestrate&lt;/strong&gt; is a 24-hour hackathon where you'll receive a real-world problem and build an agent-based solution for it. The challenge starts on &lt;strong&gt;September 12 at 6:00 PM IST&lt;/strong&gt;, and you'll have 24 hours to build and submit your solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to include this one because it's a little different from the usual hackathons where you know the problem before you start. Here, the challenge is sent to you when the hackathon begins, so you'll need to figure out the architecture, retrieval strategy, model choice, prompts, and the rest of the approach yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can also use the tools you already work with, including &lt;strong&gt;Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, or Copilot&lt;/strong&gt;. The submission includes your code, the agent's output, and your AI chat transcript, so they're looking at more than just the final result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's also an interesting part after submission: a &lt;strong&gt;30-minute voice interview with an AI judge&lt;/strong&gt;, where you'll walk through your architecture, decisions, tradeoffs, safety choices, and code. So if you're curious about what it feels like to have your AI project evaluated beyond a demo, this could be an interesting challenge to try.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are cash prizes too, with &lt;strong&gt;$1,200 for first place&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;$300 for places 2–5&lt;/strong&gt;, along with other rewards. The top three winners who build with Codex can also receive additional Codex credits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing worth knowing before signing up is that this is a &lt;strong&gt;24-hour challenge&lt;/strong&gt;, and the problem itself isn't revealed until the start. So it may be more suited to people who enjoy working under a tight deadline rather than those looking for a longer, more relaxed hackathon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prize:&lt;/strong&gt; $1,200 for 1st place | $300 for 2nd–5th place&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Format:&lt;/strong&gt; Online | &lt;strong&gt;Duration:&lt;/strong&gt; 24 hours | &lt;strong&gt;Team:&lt;/strong&gt; Solo&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Challenge Starts:&lt;/strong&gt; September 12, 2026 at 6:00 PM IST&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you need to build:&lt;/strong&gt; An AI agent that solves a real-world problem provided when the challenge begins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Evaluation:&lt;/strong&gt; Code, agent output, AI chat transcript, and a 30-minute AI judge interview.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Eligibility:&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone can participate | No prior AI experience required&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hackerrank.com/hackerrank-orchestrate-september26" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Learn More &amp;amp; Register&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


📌 Claude Campus Ambassador Program
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who it's for:&lt;/strong&gt; Students who want to help other students learn about and use AI on their campus. There are separate tracks for &lt;strong&gt;undergraduates, master's students, and PhD students/postdocs&lt;/strong&gt;, with different responsibilities for each.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What stands out:&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;strong&gt;Claude Campus Ambassador program&lt;/strong&gt; from &lt;strong&gt;Anthropic&lt;/strong&gt; is back for the 2026–27 school year, and applications are currently open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to include this because it's a little different from the usual student programs I come across. Instead of being focused mainly on learning or completing a project, you're helping build an AI community on your campus and organizing activities for other students.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For &lt;strong&gt;undergraduate students&lt;/strong&gt;, the program involves leading a &lt;strong&gt;Claude Builder Club&lt;/strong&gt;, organizing technical and non-technical workshops, running hackathons or demo nights, and sharing resources with members. Selected ambassadors receive a &lt;strong&gt;$3,600 cash stipend&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For &lt;strong&gt;master's students&lt;/strong&gt;, there's a &lt;strong&gt;Claude Campus Conversations&lt;/strong&gt; track focused more on discussions around AI and its impact on education and society. You'd organize discussion groups and events, while also helping students share and discuss the AI projects they're building.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For &lt;strong&gt;PhD students and postdocs&lt;/strong&gt;, the &lt;strong&gt;Claude Science&lt;/strong&gt; track is focused on researchers using AI in their work. This includes leading workshops for other researchers, organizing Claude for Science sessions, and engaging with Anthropic's science teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All three tracks also give ambassadors opportunities to participate in workshops led by Anthropic, connect with other ambassadors, and share feedback with Anthropic's teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few things are worth knowing before applying. The program runs for the &lt;strong&gt;full school year, from September 2026 through June 2027&lt;/strong&gt;, so this is more of an ongoing commitment than a short-term program. You also need to be &lt;strong&gt;18 or older&lt;/strong&gt; and have work authorization in your country of study.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The program is open to students &lt;strong&gt;anywhere in the world&lt;/strong&gt;, although the initial pilot for the graduate and PhD tracks may select from a smaller group of schools. You don't need to be an AI expert, but Anthropic does expect some level of AI fluency and familiarity with Claude.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stipend:&lt;/strong&gt; $3,600&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Format:&lt;/strong&gt; Campus-based | &lt;strong&gt;Program Duration:&lt;/strong&gt; September 2026 – June 2027&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tracks:&lt;/strong&gt; Claude Builder Club (Undergraduate), Claude Campus Conversations (Master's), and Claude Science (PhD/Postdocs)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Eligibility:&lt;/strong&gt; Students worldwide | &lt;strong&gt;Age:&lt;/strong&gt; 18+&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Application Deadline:&lt;/strong&gt; September 12, 2026&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cost:&lt;/strong&gt; Free to apply and participate&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://claude.com/programs/campus" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Learn More &amp;amp; Apply&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


📌 Bharat Builds Tour
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Who it's for:&lt;/strong&gt; University students in India who want to build projects through a series of online and in-person hackathons. You must be &lt;strong&gt;18 or older&lt;/strong&gt; to register on your own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What stands out:&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;strong&gt;Bharat Builds Tour&lt;/strong&gt;, organized by &lt;strong&gt;WeMakeDevs in collaboration with AWS Builder Center&lt;/strong&gt;, is a six-stop hackathon series where students can participate online from anywhere in India. Each stop has its own hackathon, and you can take part in as many of the six as you'd like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to include this one even though it's geographically limited because there is a &lt;strong&gt;hiring component&lt;/strong&gt; that I don't come across very often in student hackathons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For students graduating in &lt;strong&gt;2027 or 2028&lt;/strong&gt;, some of the top projects can be eligible for a &lt;strong&gt;fast-track interview opportunity with Amazon's University Talent Acquisition Team&lt;/strong&gt; for available internship and full-time roles. The fast-track means the usual screening round is skipped and the project is used in its place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That doesn't mean winning a hackathon automatically gets you an Amazon interview, though. The rules say that being shortlisted for a prize and being put forward for an interview are separate decisions, and the interview opportunity is also subject to Amazon's eligibility requirements, available roles, and business needs. So I'd definitely read the terms before counting on the hiring part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tour itself includes &lt;strong&gt;six hackathons&lt;/strong&gt;, with the first one, &lt;strong&gt;First Commit&lt;/strong&gt;, taking place September 17–20. Every stop has an online option for students across India, while the city hosting that particular stop also has limited in-person seats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can participate &lt;strong&gt;solo or in teams of up to four&lt;/strong&gt;, and you don't need previous cloud experience. Each project does need to use AWS, and submissions include a public repository, a short demo video, and a write-up explaining the project and where AWS is used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are also prizes across the tour, with a total prize value of &lt;strong&gt;₹1 crore in gadgets and cloud credits&lt;/strong&gt;. Participants can also get access to AWS learning resources, credits, a certification voucher, mentors, and a WeMakeDevs certificate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing worth knowing is that &lt;strong&gt;one registration covers all six stops&lt;/strong&gt;, but you still need to check in to each hackathon as it opens. You'll also need an AWS Builder Center profile and verified student status before your entry can be scored.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prize Pool:&lt;/strong&gt; Prizes worth ₹1 crore across six hackathons&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Format:&lt;/strong&gt; Online across India + limited in-person seats&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;First Hackathon:&lt;/strong&gt; September 17–20, 2026&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Team Size:&lt;/strong&gt; Solo or up to 4 people&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Eligibility:&lt;/strong&gt; University students in India, 18+&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hiring:&lt;/strong&gt; Top projects from eligible 2027 and 2028 students may receive a fast-track interview opportunity with Amazon&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cost:&lt;/strong&gt; Free&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.wemakedevs.org/aws" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Learn More &amp;amp; Register&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


📚 Resources Worth Checking Out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every useful find comes with an application deadline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's one resource worth checking out this week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The System Design Primer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If system design is something you're trying to learn, this is one resource I came across that is worth keeping around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;System Design Primer&lt;/strong&gt; is a free, open-source GitHub repository with a collection of system design topics, interview questions, real-world architectures, and other resources. It also has a study guide that gives you an idea of what to focus on depending on how much time you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It covers things like &lt;strong&gt;scalability, latency, availability, consistency, DNS, CDNs, load balancers, databases, caching, microservices, and distributed systems&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I liked here is that it also has actual system design questions to work through, such as &lt;strong&gt;Pastebin, Twitter, a web crawler, a social network, and scaling a system to millions of users on AWS&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also a section on how to approach a system design interview, starting with requirements and constraints, then moving into the high-level design, core components, scaling, and trade-offs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So if you're just getting started with system design, or preparing for interviews and don't know what topics to go through first, this could be a good place to start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Format:&lt;/strong&gt; Free, open-source GitHub repository&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Covers:&lt;/strong&gt; System design, scalability, databases, caching, distributed systems, networking, microservices, and more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Also includes:&lt;/strong&gt; System design interview questions, real-world architectures, and Anki flashcards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Explore the System Design Primer&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




