&lt;p&gt;No third-party provider should be assumed to have the ability to guarantee Facebook's future decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why “Old” Does Not Mean “Ban-Proof”&lt;br&gt;
One of the most misleading assumptions about old accounts is that they cannot be restricted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Account age does not make an account immune to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security checks&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Temporary restrictions&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Content enforcement&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Login challenges&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Policy actions&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Facebook may restrict certain activity when systems detect behavior that appears abusive or unusual. Current Facebook pages can also display temporary blocks for suspected misuse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Therefore, users should focus on legitimate activity rather than assuming account age provides protection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Account Recovery and Backup Planning&lt;br&gt;
Recovery planning is an important part of responsible Facebook management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users should make sure they have access to legitimate recovery methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful practices can include:&lt;/p&gt;

